{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And also the nation that they shall serve</b> — And also to include four exiles because they enslaved Israel, in the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 15:14). And according to the plain sense, one may say: just as I have judged your children in exile and affliction for sin, so also the nation that they shall serve I will judge for the violence that they do to them. And the correct interpretation in my eyes is that the reason for \"and also\" (וגם) is: even though I have decreed upon your seed to be strangers in a land not theirs, and they shall serve them and afflict them, even so I will judge the nation that they serve for what they do to them, and they shall not be exempt on account of having fulfilled My decree. And the reason is as Scripture says (Zechariah 1:14–15), \"And I am zealous for Jerusalem and for Zion with a great zeal, and a great wrath — I am wrathful against the nations that are at ease, for when I was wrathful a little, they helped toward evil,\" and it says (Isaiah 47:6), \"I was wrathful against My people, I profaned My inheritance,\" etc. And so it was in Egypt: they added to the evil, for they cast their children into the river and embittered their lives, and they plotted to wipe out their name entirely. And this is the meaning of \"I will judge\" (דן אנכי), that I will bring them to judgment whether they did as was decreed upon them or added further evil to them. And this is what Jethro said: \"for in the very thing in which they schemed against them\" (Exodus 18:11), for it was the scheming (זדון) that brought upon them the great punishment that destroyed them from the world; and likewise, \"for you know that they dealt presumptuously against them\" (Nehemiah 9:10). And the Rav gave a reason in the Book of Knowledge (Laws of Repentance 6:5), namely that no specific individual was designated, and all those who did evil to Israel — had any one of them not wished to, the power was in his hand, since no specific person was decreed upon. But his words do not sit well with me, for even if He decreed that one of all the nations would do evil to them thus and so, and that one came and carried out the decree of the Holy One, blessed be He, he would be rewarded with a mitzvah — and what reason would there be in his words? For when a king commands that the inhabitants of a certain city perform a certain task, the one who is negligent and casts the burden upon others is guilty of oppression and sins against himself, while the one who performs it derives favor from him. All the more so, since Scripture says \"and also the nation that they shall serve\" — meaning that the entire nation enslaved them, and Israel went to Egypt of their own accord. Rather, the reason is as I have written. And our Sages have already mentioned this matter: they said in Shemot Rabbah (30:15), a parable of a man who said to his son, \"Do business with so-and-so, but do not mistreat him.\" He went and did business with him; even though he served him for free, he did not refrain from mistreating him. When the father was reconciled with his son, he decreed death upon his son's tormentor. So too the Holy One, blessed be He, decreed that Israel would be enslaved in Egypt; they rose up against them and enslaved them with force. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: You should have treated them as servants and had them do your work; \"I was wrathful a little, and they helped toward evil\" (Zechariah 1:15) — end of their language. And it is a clear matter that casting their children into the river is not included in \"and they shall serve them and afflict them,\" but is rather their complete uprooting. And likewise what they said at the outset: \"Come, let us deal wisely with him, lest he multiply\" (Exodus 1:10) is not included in servitude and affliction — apart from what they added of their own in affliction: \"and they embittered their lives with hard service,\" etc. And that is what Scripture says (Deuteronomy 26:7), \"And He saw our affliction, and our toil, and our oppression.\" Know and understand that a man who has been inscribed and sealed on Rosh HaShanah for death — the bandits who kill him are not exempt on the grounds that they carried out what was decreed upon him; he is a wicked man and shall die for his iniquity, and his blood is sought from the hand of the murderer. But when a decree goes forth through a prophet, there are rules governing the one who carries it out: if he heard it and wished to do the will of his Creator in accordance with what was decreed, there is no sin upon him, but he even has merit in it, as He said concerning Jehu (II Kings 10:30), \"Because you have done well in doing what is right in My eyes, according to all that was in My heart you have done to the house of Ahab; your sons of the fourth generation shall sit upon the throne of Israel.\" But if he heard the commandment and killed him out of hatred or to despoil him, he is liable to punishment, for his intent was to sin and it constitutes a transgression for him. And thus Scripture says concerning Sennacherib (Isaiah 10:5), \"Woe to Assyria, the rod of My anger... against a godless nation I send him, and against a people of My wrath I command him,\" and Scripture says, \"But he does not so intend, and his heart does not so think, for to destroy is in his heart,\" and therefore He punished him in the end, as it is said (Isaiah 10:12), \"And it shall come to pass, when HaShem has finished all His work on Mount Zion and on Jerusalem, I will punish the fruit of the arrogant heart of the king of Assyria and the glory of his haughty eyes,\" etc. And concerning Babylon He said (Jeremiah 50:17), \"Israel is a scattered sheep; lions drove it away — the first one devoured it: the king of Assyria; and this last one ground its bones: Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon. Therefore thus says HaShem... I am bringing punishment upon the king of Babylon and upon his land, as I brought punishment upon the king of Assyria\" — and behold, this is proof that the king of Assyria was punished for the evil he did to Israel. Now Nebuchadnezzar heard that the prophets were unanimously calling him to destroy Jerusalem, and he and all his people were commanded concerning this by the prophet, as it is written (Jeremiah 25:9), \"Behold, I am sending for Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon, My servant, and I will bring them against this land and against its inhabitants and against all these nations round about, and I will devote them to destruction,\" and it is written (Jeremiah 32:28), \"Behold, I am giving this city into the hand of the Chaldeans and into the hand of Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon, and they shall burn the city with fire,\" and concerning the Temple itself He said (Jeremiah 26:6), \"And I will make this house like Shiloh,\" and they know that it is the commandment of HaShem, as Nebuzaradan said to Jeremiah (Jeremiah 40:2), \"HaShem your Elohim spoke this evil concerning this place, and HaShem brought it and did as He spoke, because you sinned against HaShem.\" Even so, all the Chaldeans were punished in the end, and this was for two reasons: the first, that he too intended to destroy all the earth to magnify his dominion, as it is written of him (Isaiah 13:11), \"And I will put an end to the arrogance of the insolent, and the haughtiness of tyrants I will humble,\" and it is written (Isaiah 14:13), \"And you said in your heart, 'I will ascend to heaven,'\" etc., and it is written, \"I will ascend above the heights of the cloud, I will make myself like the Most High,\" and it is written of his nation (Isaiah 47:8), \"You who say in your heart, 'I am, and there is none besides me,'\" and Habakkuk said of him (Habakkuk 2:9), \"Woe to him who gets evil gain for his house, to set his nest on high,\" etc. — and behold, this is like the punishment of Sennacherib. Therefore Scripture says (Jeremiah 50:18), \"Therefore thus says HaShem: Behold, I am bringing punishment upon the king of Babylon and upon his land, as I brought punishment upon the king of Assyria.\" But there was another punishment upon the king of Babylon: he added to the decree and did exceedingly great evil to Israel, as it says of him (Isaiah 47:6), \"I was wrathful against My people, I profaned My inheritance, and I gave them into your hand; you showed them no mercy; upon the aged you made your yoke very heavy,\" and therefore a doubled and redoubled punishment came upon him: his seed was utterly destroyed, and Babylon shall have neither name nor surviving remnant of offspring (Isaiah 14:22), and his city was destroyed forever, as it is said (Isaiah 13:19–20), \"And Babylon, the glory of kingdoms, the splendor of the pride of the Chaldeans, shall be like the overthrow of Elohim of Sodom and Gomorrah. It shall never be inhabited, nor dwelt in from generation to generation; and desert creatures shall lie down there, and satyrs shall dance there,\" and Scripture says of it (Jeremiah 51:11), \"For it is the vengeance of HaShem, the vengeance of His temple,\" and it is written (Jeremiah 51:35–36), \"My violence and my flesh is upon Babylon, shall say the inhabitant of Zion, and my blood is upon the inhabitants of Chaldea, shall say Jerusalem. Therefore thus says HaShem: Behold, I will plead your cause and take vengeance for you,\" and many verses like these."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:30:36.741090437Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}